From 0baf8ca8c9a756424eb4c1f629958a8bfc3dce90 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ZCloudium <zcloudium@local>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 20:33:08 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] =?UTF-8?q?zcloudium:=20durable=20server-side=20remote?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20sessions=20=E2=80=94=20the=20browser=20is=20disposable,=20th?=
 =?UTF-8?q?e=20work=20is=20not?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A browser refresh used to dispose the SSH connection and kill the
runtime deployed on the target: background tasks died with the tab,
and restoring a persisted conversation timed out. Two independent
defects, both fixed.

1. The one-shot ticket maps are replaced by a durable registry
   (remoteSessionRegistry.ts): live connections keyed by target
   identity (reused instead of redeployed), logical sessions scoped per
   workspace and multiplexed onto their connection, browser sockets
   attached and detached without ever disposing the session. Same-target
   connects racing on different scopes serialize on one SSH channel —
   the loser disposes its duplicate and multiplexes on the winner — and
   handleRemoteClose sweeps by connection identity so a lost race can
   never strand sessions. The connection dies only when the remote side
   closes (onDidRemoteClose), when the operator disposes it, or on
   server restart.

2. The runtime deployed in desktop-attached-remote mode asks its host
   for session runtime preferences on cold resume (15 s timeout).
   Desktop answers from its setting service; the web server never wired
   that, so every persisted-session resume failed with
   fault.subscribe.resumeFailed. The same bridge is now wired
   server-side from the standalone server's setting service.

3. The web entry passes autoReconnectRemoteWorkspaces: restored remote
   tabs re-attach themselves on load — instantly when the server-side
   session is still alive (dedupe), as a real reconnect when it died.
   Desktop keeps its own host-side reattach path.
